item.text:
Accepted set for Chapter 3B points to a recurring pattern of state-of-siege abuse, protest/dissent repression, weak detention oversight, and severe civilian protection failures in the east. I kept only sources that added something materially distinct.

REUSE_JSON: {"evidence_ids":["E0013"],"lenses":["3B.5","3B.9","3B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"DRC: One year on, 'State of Siege' used as a tool to crush dissent","publisher":"Amnesty International","publication_date":"2022-05-10","url":"https://www.amnesty.org/en/latest/news/2022/05/drc-one-year-on-state-of-siege/","claim":"Amnesty said the state of siege in North Kivu and Ituri had been used to crush dissent, with two activists killed by security forces, dozens arbitrarily detained, and military courts used against critics.","locator":"lines 61-70 and 79-106","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous rights briefing with direct reporting and interview-based findings.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 state-of-siege context","ruler_attribution":"Directly relevant to Tshisekedi because he maintained the state of siege and was urged to lift its restrictions.","contrary_evidence":["The same briefing notes the government said the measure aimed to protect civilians and combat armed groups."],"lenses":["3B.5","3B.8","3B.9","3B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"2023 Country Report on Human Rights Practices: Democratic Republic of the Congo","publisher":"USDOS - U.S. Department of State (via ecoi.net)","publication_date":"best available","url":"https://www.ecoi.net/en/document/2107668.html","claim":"The report says 2022 in the DRC featured arbitrary killings, disappearances, torture, arbitrary arrest or detention, political prisoners, serious media restrictions, substantial interference with peaceful assembly under the state of siege, serious government corruption, gender-based violence, and ethnic violence; it also says the government took some steps to investigate and punish officials.","locator":"lines 36-41","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official U.S. human-rights report reproduced by ecoi.net with direct country-specific findings.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retrospective 2022 country assessment","ruler_attribution":"State-level conduct during Tshisekedi's tenure; not all abuses are proven personal orders.","contrary_evidence":["The report also notes some government investigative and punitive steps."],"lenses":["3B.1","3B.2","3B.4","3B.5","3B.8","3B.9","3B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"2023 Country Report on Human Rights Practices: Democratic Republic of the Congo","publisher":"USDOS - U.S. Department of State (via ecoi.net)","publication_date":"best available","url":"https://www.ecoi.net/en/document/2107668.html","claim":"On August 30 in Goma, government security forces used deadly and disproportionate force against a banned protest, reportedly killing 56 demonstrators and arresting 158 people, after which the governor of North Kivu was relieved of his post and an investigation was launched.","locator":"lines 46-47","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official U.S. report gives a specific incident, casualty count, and immediate state response.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 domestic protest episode","ruler_attribution":"Security forces acted under Tshisekedi's government; the report documents the state response but not a personal order.","contrary_evidence":["The report notes a government investigation and some removals/arrests after the incident."],"lenses":["3B.1","3B.2","3B.8","3B.9","3B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"2023 Country Report on Human Rights Practices: Democratic Republic of the Congo","publisher":"USDOS - U.S. Department of State (via ecoi.net)","publication_date":"best available","url":"https://www.ecoi.net/en/document/2107668.html","claim":"The report says police in Maniema arbitrarily arrested three human-rights defenders for protesting mistreatment of the ethnic Twa community, and the chief prosecutor later released them for lack of evidence.","locator":"lines 82-83","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official report with a named incident, date window, and outcome.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Late-2022 / early-2023 follow-through on 2022 repression patterns","ruler_attribution":"Relevant to the administration's handling of protest and vulnerable-group defense, though the incident is provincial.","contrary_evidence":["The later release indicates some remedy, albeit after arbitrary arrest."],"lenses":["3B.2","3B.7","3B.8","3B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"2023 Country Report on Human Rights Practices: Democratic Republic of the Congo","publisher":"USDOS - U.S. Department of State (via ecoi.net)","publication_date":"best available","url":"https://www.ecoi.net/en/document/2107668.html","claim":"The report says political prisoners and detainees were mostly people arrested under defamation laws for criticizing officials or during protests, and that Jean-Marc Kabund remained jailed despite a court order for house arrest.","locator":"lines 94-100","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official report with direct detail on political detention and due-process failure.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"2022 political detention and protest-repression context","ruler_attribution":"Directly tied to Tshisekedi-era detention practice and political-space restrictions.","contrary_evidence":["The report also notes some prisoners had international-access monitoring."],"lenses":["3B.1","3B.2","3B.4","3B.8","3B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Democratic Republic of the Congo: Freedom in the World 2023 Country Report","publisher":"Freedom House","publication_date":"2023","url":"https://freedomhouse.org/country/democratic-republic-congo/freedom-world/2023","claim":"Freedom House reports that violent protests against MONUSCO in July 2022 killed 36 people nationwide, that opposition leader Jean-Marc Kabund was arrested in August for allegedly insulting President Tshisekedi, and that assembly rights were curtailed in state-of-siege areas.","locator":"lines 85-87 and 189-191","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent country report with specific 2022 incidents and institutional analysis.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 developments summarized in a 2023 report","ruler_attribution":"Relevant to Tshisekedi's domestic-security and protest-response record.","contrary_evidence":["The report also notes some opposition figures were released and some exiles returned under Tshisekedi."],"lenses":["3B.5","3B.8","3B.9","3B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"UNHCR deeply concerned by renewed violence displacing thousands in North Kivu, DR Congo","publisher":"UNHCR","publication_date":"2022-05-27","url":"https://www.unhcr.org/news/briefing-notes/unhcr-deeply-concerned-renewed-violence-displacing-thousands-north-kivu-dr","claim":"UNHCR said at least 170,000 civilians had been displaced since the November 2021 escalation, and warned that women and girls faced sexual violence, extortion, and repeated displacement.","locator":"lines 392-399","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Authoritative humanitarian briefing with concrete displacement and protection figures.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 conflict and displacement context","ruler_attribution":"Indirect state-protection context under Tshisekedi's government.","contrary_evidence":["The source describes harm from armed clashes rather than a direct presidential order."],"lenses":["3B.7","3B.9","3B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"In eastern Democratic Republic of the Congo (DRC), the surge in violence has driven thousands of displaced people to the outskirts of Goma","publisher":"ICRC","publication_date":"2022-06-01","url":"https://www.icrc.org/en/document/eastern-democratic-republic-congo-drc-surge-violence-has-driven-thousands-displaced-people","claim":"The ICRC said fighting in Rutshuru forced more than 72,000 people to flee in one week and urged all combatants to protect civilians and allow humanitarian workers to reach them.","locator":"lines 178-189","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary humanitarian field release with exact displacement figures and civilian-protection appeal.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 eastern-conflict surge","ruler_attribution":"Relevant to the government's civilian-protection responsibilities under Tshisekedi.","contrary_evidence":["The release does not assign each abuse to the president personally."],"lenses":["3B.7","3B.9","3B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Les enfants sous attaque dans l'escalade du conflit à l'est de la RDC","publisher":"UNICEF","publication_date":"2022-06-25","url":"https://www.unicef.org/drcongo/communiques-presse/enfants-sous-attaque-escalade-conflit-est-rdc","claim":"UNICEF said armed attacks in Rutshuru killed four children, more than 158,000 people had been displaced since late March, and 652 children were separated from their parents or guardians.","locator":"lines 71-76","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary UNICEF country-office release with concrete child-protection figures.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 child-protection emergency","ruler_attribution":"Directly relevant to Tshisekedi's responsibility to protect vulnerable groups.","contrary_evidence":["The release describes armed clashes rather than a direct presidential order."],"lenses":["3B.7","3B.9","3B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"DR Congo: Resurgent M23 Rebels Target Civilians","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2022-07-25","url":"https://www.hrw.org/news/2022/07/25/dr-congo-resurgent-m23-rebels-target-civilians","claim":"Human Rights Watch said M23 summarily killed at least 29 civilians since mid-June 2022 and that the fighting forced nearly 200,000 people to flee their homes.","locator":"lines 604-608","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Well-documented independent rights report based on interviews and field reporting.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 escalation in eastern DRC","ruler_attribution":"Indirectly relevant to Tshisekedi's protection record and failure-to-prevent context.","contrary_evidence":["The killings were attributed to M23, not to Tshisekedi's direct orders."],"lenses":["3B.7","3B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Democratic Republic of the Congo","publisher":"Office of the Special Representative of the Secretary-General for Children and Armed Conflict","publication_date":"2022-12-12","url":"https://childrenandarmedconflict.un.org/where-we-work/democratic-republic-of-the-congo/","claim":"The DRC profile recorded 305 children killed or maimed, 557 children victims of sexual violence, and 1,192 children abducted in the year, and said violations by government forces increased, especially killings, maiming, and attacks on schools and hospitals, while some commanders signed child-protection commitments and 430 children were released.","locator":"grave violations and developments/concerns sections","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official UN monitoring page with country-specific 2022 grave-violations statistics and government-related findings.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"2022 child-protection record","ruler_attribution":"Relevant to Tshisekedi's responsibility for state-force conduct and child protection.","contrary_evidence":["The page also reports some government commitments and releases, so the record is mixed rather than uniformly negative."],"lenses":["3B.1","3B.2","3B.7","3B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Congo arrests opposition leader Kabund","publisher":"Reuters","publication_date":"2022-08-09","url":"https://www.yahoo.com/news/congo-arrests-opposition-leader-kabund-165127790.html","claim":"Reuters reported that opposition leader Jean-Marc Kabund was arrested after a controversy over remarks about President Tshisekedi, and that his lawyers said he was accused of contempt of the head of state.","locator":"lead paragraph and lawyer quotation","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous Reuters reporting on a politically sensitive arrest.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 dissent-repression incident","ruler_attribution":"Directly relevant to Tshisekedi-era treatment of a former ally turned opposition figure.","contrary_evidence":["The article reports allegations and charges, not a final adjudication of guilt."],"lenses":["3B.8","3B.9","3B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Journalists targeted by fighters in eastern DRC's Nord-Kivu province","publisher":"Reporters Without Borders","publication_date":"2022-06-24","url":"https://rsf.org/en/journalists-targeted-fighters-eastern-drc-s-nord-kivu-province","claim":"RSF said both government forces and M23 rebels were targeting journalists in North Kivu, that a community radio station had been ransacked after the Bunagana attack, and that journalists were facing threats and cyber-harassment.","locator":"article body and quoted RSF Africa-desk statement","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent press-freedom monitor with direct country-specific reporting.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 media-safety context","ruler_attribution":"Relevant to the administration's failure to protect journalists, though abuses were perpetrated by multiple actors.","contrary_evidence":["The report also assigns some threats to M23 and other armed actors, not only the government."],"lenses":["3B.2","3B.8","3B.10"]}

Search ledger:
- Candidate documents found: about 50.
- Documents opened: 12 accessible source pages, plus supplemental find views on opened pages.
- Duplicate families removed: 14.
- Irrelevant or out-of-period items culled: 8.
- Inaccessible items: 6.
- Accepted distinct URLs: 10 new URLs retained, plus 1 reused prior URL.

Material gaps:
- I still do not have a clean primary record for a central, national-level accountability mechanism that resolved abuse in 2022.
- The strongest evidence is on repression, arrests, protest killings, and civilian-protection failure; I did not find a comparable amount of direct evidence of positive reform implementation.
- I do not yet have a direct, archived DRC government source on the full scope of investigations, prosecutions, or discipline after the August 30 Goma protest deaths.
- Provincial incidents are well documented, but national command attribution remains partly inferential rather than directly ordered.

Next best directions:
1. Pull direct MONUSCO or UNJHRO incident reports for the August 30 Goma protest and any later investigation findings.
2. Pull any official DRC justice ministry or prosecutor records on the Kabund case, the Maniema arrests, and post-protest accountability.
3. Pull a 2022 or early-2023 MONUSCO human-rights bulletin covering North Kivu, Ituri, and Maniema for a tighter state-aligned abuse and remedy record.
4. Pull any parliamentary or presidential decree text clarifying the exact scope and renewal of the state of siege in 2022.
5. If you want a tighter protest/dissent sub-dossier, add local media or NGO reporting on the July MONUSCO protests and the Bunagana-related assembly restrictions.
